==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|stroggell|strogell||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|struglen}}, {{m|enm|stroglen}}, {{m|enm|strogelen}}, of obscure origin; perhaps from a variant of {{m|enm|*strokelen}}, {{m|enm|*stroukelen}} {{gloss|> English {{l|en|stroll}}}}, from {{der|en|dum|struyckelen}} {{gloss|"to stumble, trip, falter"; > Modern Dutch {{m|nl|struikelen}}}}, the frequentative form of {{der|en|odt|*strūkon||to stumble}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*strūkōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*strūkēną||to be stiff}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*strug-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ster-||to be stiff; to bristle, strut, stumble, fall}}, 

Cognate with {{cog|sco|strugil||to struggle, grapple, contend}}. Also related to {{cog|gml|strûkelen}} {{gloss|"to stumble"; > {{cog|nds|strükeln}}}}, {{cog|goh|strūhhēn}}, {{m|goh|strūhhōn}} {{gloss|"to stumble, trip, tumble, go astray"; > {{cog|de|strauchen}}, {{m|de|straucheln}}}}.

Alternative etymology derives the base of ''struggle'' from {{der|en|non|strúgr||arrogance, pride, spitefulness, ill-will}} + {{af|en|-le|pos1=frequentative suffix}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*strūkaz||stiff, rigid}}, ultimately from the same Proto-Indo-European root above, which would make it cognate with dialectal {{cog|sv|strug||contention, strife, discord}}, {{cog|no|stru||obstinate, unruly}}, {{cog|da|struende||reluctantly}}, {{cog|sco|strug||difficulty, perplexity, a laborious task}}.

